Me again! `fetch` uses the `globalDispatcher` set by `undici`. This dispatcher uses an `Agent` with its defaults. Under-the-hood, `Agent` creates, depending on the configuration passed, an instance of either a `Pool` or `Client`, if entirely based on the defaults, a `Client` is made (it depends on the number of `connections` passed). Usually, this configuration works perfectly for many cases, but for more complex use cases, you can alter it by passing an `Agent` instance to the `setGlobalDispatcher`, another option is to do the same but pass a `dispatcher-like` object to fetch (usually a Pool or Client instance), e.g. `fetch('url', { dispatcher })`. H2 support (H3 is not in Node.js yet) is experimental, and can be used by creating a client or agent with the flag `allowH2: true`. Hope is pretty clear, but a PR is welcome to improve our documentation around those topics 🙂

